Independence and non-affiliation
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
This is an INDEPENDENT, UNOFFICIAL project. It is a personal, devotional, and
cultural tribute to the Vatican's AI-ethics work (the Rome Call for AI Ethics,
Antiqua et Nova, and the encyclical Magnifica humanitas). It is NOT affiliated
with, sponsored by, or endorsed by the Holy See, the Pontifical Academy for
Life, the RenAIssance Foundation, the Dicastery for the Doctrine of the Faith,
the Dicastery for Culture and Education, or Anthropic. Anthropic is not a
signatory of the Rome Call for AI Ethics. Any names, documents, or institutions
referenced are cited for factual and devotional context only.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Included prayer texts (public domain)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The files under prayers/ are public-domain liturgical texts. No copyright is
claimed over them by this project.

- prayers/pater-noster.txt — Latin "Pater Noster" (Vulgate / Catechism wording).
- prayers/padre-nuestro.txt — Spanish "Padre Nuestro" (standard Catholic
  traditional wording).
- prayers/our-father.txt — English "Our Father" (traditional wording from the
  King James Bible, 1611, Matthew 6:9-13, and the Book of Common Prayer, 1662).

The traditional KJV/1662 English wording was chosen deliberately over the modern
ELLC/ICET (1988) ecumenical wording to avoid that text's "© ELLC, 1988"
attribution ambiguity in a permissively licensed repository.

These texts are included as optional, swappable data (disabled by default). A
fork may replace them with another prayer, a secular quotation, or nothing,
without modifying any code.
